// @run
// @log 0
// The `null` TYPE spelled ON ITS OWN, rather than as a union member.
//
// Why this file exists. `null` is overwhelmingly written as a union MEMBER
// (`T | null`), and a union member is consumed by the union arm of annotation
// resolution, which recognises it in place — the resolver's standalone `null`
// LEAF never runs for it. This file is that leaf's witness.
//
// If the standalone `null` spelling ever stops resolving, the element poisons
// the array, the annotation becomes `unknown type 'null[]'`, and the checker
// rejects the program. The element position is deliberate: it reaches the leaf
// through the resolver's recursion (array-of), not only at an annotation's root.
const xs: null[] = []
print(xs.length)
